WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.380
When we represent
information, such as an image,

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.420
digitally, it means we must
slice it up into tiny chunks.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.080
This allows us to send an image
as a sequence of color symbols.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.640
And these colors can be
represented as unique numbers

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.575
using some code.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.050
Consider the
following challenge.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.724
Alice and Bob can transmit and
receive messages in binary.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.040
They charge their
customers one penny

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.300
per bit to use their system.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.340
And a regular customer arrives
who wants to send a message.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.530
And their messages are
1,000 symbols long.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.770
The meaning of the messages
is completely unknown.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:57.850
Normally, this is sent with a
standard two-bit code, which

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.330
results in charging
for 2,000 bits.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.470
However, Alice and Bob
already did some analysis

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.310
on this customer
before and determined

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:10.270
that the probability of
each symbol in the message

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:11.940
is different.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.620
Can they use these
known probabilities

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:19.330
to compress the transmission
and increase their profits?

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.685
What's the optimal
coding strategy?

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.550
David Huffman famously
provided the optimal strategy,

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.540
which he published
in 1952, and based

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.770
on building a binary
tree from the bottom up.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.290
To begin, we can
list all symbols

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:41.560
at the bottom, which
we can call nodes.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.820
And then we find the two
least probable nodes,

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.200
in this case B and C,
and merge them into one,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.690
and add the
probabilities together.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:57.470
Then we repeat with the
next two least likely nodes,

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:02.110
and continue merging until you
have a single node at the top.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:06.730
Finally, we label the edges
in this tree with 0 or 1

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.470
in any order.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.280
Now the code for
each letter is just

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.210
the path from the top of the
tree to the given letter.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:17.760
So for A, it's just
one edge, or 1.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.000
This is now known
as a Huffman coding,

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.470
and for examples of
the following type,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.610
you cannot beat it.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.020
Go ahead and try.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:30.440
For example, if you shorten
the code for D to just 0,

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:40.330
then a message 011 could perhaps
mean DAA, or maybe just B.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:44.570
So for this to work, you'd
need to introduce letter spaces

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:49.360
which cancel out any
savings during transmission.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.400
Now how far does this
compress the message

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.650
compared to the
original 2,000 bits?

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.910
Well, we just need to calculate
the number of bits per letter

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.140
on average.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.230
So we multiply the length of
each code times the probability

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.060
of occurrence and
add them together,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.770
which results in an average
length of 1.75 bits per symbol

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.480
on average.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.590
That means with
this Huffman coding,

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:17.410
we can expect to
compress the messages

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:23.220
from 2,000 bits to 1,750 bits.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.440
And Claude Shannon
was the first to claim

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.830
that the limit of
compression will always

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.150
be the entropy of
the message source.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.330
As the entropy, or
uncertainty of our source

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:45.570
decreases due to known
statistical structure,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.655
the ability to
compress increases.

00:03:47.655 --> 00:03:50.910
[MORSE CODE BEEPING]

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:56.330
While if the entropy increases
due to unpredictability,

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:59.526
our ability to
compress decreases.

00:03:59.526 --> 00:04:02.720
[MORSE CODE BEEPING]

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.450
If we want to compress
it beyond entropy,

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:10.230
we must necessarily throw away
information in our messages.

